!deck: Programming
!type: Basic
!tags: Programming Regex

!id: pY2WSHjQTa
!front: What is an anchor in a regex?
!back: An anchor is a zero-width assertion about position. It does not consume characters; it checks facts such as "start of input", "end of input", or "word boundary".
~~~

!id: DPuIYLOiKW
!front: What does the start anchor `^` assert?
!back: `^` asserts that the current position is at the start of the input, or at the start of a line in multiline mode. It consumes no characters.
~~~

!id: 7fOmc3DFUq
!front: What does the end anchor `$` assert?
!back: `$` asserts that the current position is at the end of the input, or at the end of a line in multiline mode. It consumes no characters.
~~~

!id: zLsRhXnqz7
!front: Why does anchoring matter when using regex for validation?
!back: Without anchors or full-match mode, a regex may succeed by matching only a valid substring inside invalid surrounding text. Validation normally requires the whole input to satisfy the pattern.
~~~

!id: zSGCKhaRME
!front: What does `^\d+$` assert about an input string?
!back: It asserts that the whole input consists of one or more digit characters: `^` fixes the start, `\d+` matches at least one digit, and `$` fixes the end.
~~~

!id: 5MLBu22AGL
!front: What is a word boundary `\b`?
!back: A word boundary is a zero-width assertion for a position between a word character and a non-word character, or between a word character and the edge of the input.
~~~

!id: fOSqhhkaKN
!front: Does `\bcat\b` match the `cat` inside `concatenate`? Why or why not?
!back: No. The `cat` inside `concatenate` is adjacent to word characters on both sides, so the positions around it are not word boundaries.
~~~

!id: 6aJxSCGbJn
!front: Why does `\b` not add a character to the matched text?
!back: `\b` is zero-width. It checks whether the current position is a word boundary, but it does not consume any input character.
~~~
